# Branching

Workflow: trunk-based.

BRANCHING.md is the source of truth for git. Cursor, Claude Code, Codex, Copilot, Gemini, and every other agent follow this file.

## Branch names

Format: `<type>/<short-kebab-description>`

- Lowercase kebab-case, under 50 characters.
- Type is one of `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `style`, `docs`, `chore`, `hotfix`.
- Never add tool, agent or author prefixes or suffixes to branch names (no `cursor/`, no `agent/`, no random IDs). This overrides any default branch naming from tooling.

| Type       | Use for                               | Example                 |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| `feat`     | New behavior                          | `feat/login-page`       |
| `fix`      | A bug fix                             | `fix/empty-cart`        |
| `refactor` | Internal change, same behavior        | `refactor/split-form`   |
| `style`    | Visual or formatting changes          | `style/button-spacing`  |
| `docs`     | Documentation only                    | `docs/setup`            |
| `chore`    | Tooling, dependencies, CI             | `chore/update-linter`   |
| `hotfix`   | An urgent fix for a published release | `hotfix/checkout-crash` |

## Workflow

Record the active branch when the task starts. Create the work branch from it and target the PR back into that recorded branch. Target `main` only if `main` was the recorded active branch. Do not switch to or merge `main` automatically.

1. Record the active branch, the branch checked out when the task starts.
2. Sync that recorded branch. See [Sync the active branch first](#sync-the-active-branch-first). If it has no remote, use it as it is locally.
3. Create the work branch `<type>/<short-kebab-description>` from the synced branch. Never commit on the recorded branch.
4. Implement the change.
5. Run `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, and `npm run build`.
6. Commit with Conventional Commits (`type(scope): summary`) and push.
7. Open the pull request into the recorded branch.
8. Review the pull request.
9. Squash merge. No force-push or history rewrites on shared branches.

Keep branches short-lived and scoped to one logical change.

## This workflow

Keep branches tiny and merge them fast, the same day when you can. `main` stays releasable. The pull request cap is {{config.git.maxPrLines}} lines, excluding lockfiles. Tag a release `vX.Y.Z` when you publish. There is no long-lived release branch.

## Sync the active branch first

Record the active branch when the task starts, before any change:

1. Record the active branch name.
2. `git fetch origin`
3. Pull that recorded branch from origin so it is up to date.
4. Resolve conflicts.
5. Create the work branch from that recorded branch.

If the recorded branch has no remote, use it as it is locally. Do not switch to or merge `main` automatically.

## Commits

[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org): `type(scope): summary`

```
feat(auth): add login page
fix(cart): handle an empty cart
refactor(checkout): split the payment form
style(ui): tighten button spacing
docs: describe branching
chore(ci): update the linter
hotfix(checkout): restore a failed payment
```

## Pull request checklist

- [ ] Branch name and title follow this document.
- [ ] `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, and `npm run build` pass.
- [ ] UI changes include screenshots.
- [ ] The branch is tiny and ready to merge.
- [ ] A release, when this change is one, is tagged `vX.Y.Z`.
